gamma: don't let a refused gamma control claim the monitor forever

onGetGammaControl keeps every CGammaControl it constructs, including
ones the constructor refuses (duplicate controller, no output, gamma
size 0). The constructor assigned m_monitor before those checks and
registered its destroy handlers only after them, so a refused controller
stayed in m_gammaControllers for good, still pointing at the monitor.

From then on that output's gamma was dead until the compositor
restarted: every new get_gamma_control matched the zombie in the
duplicate check and got an immediate `failed` (no gamma_size first),
and applyGammaToState() picked the zombie -- first match, no table set
-- reset the LUT to empty and broke out of the loop.

Observed on a laptop (Meteor Lake, eDP-1): GAMMA_LUT stayed 0 on the
CRTC through any number of night-mode toggles and DMS restarts, and an
unrelated client (gammastep) got the same immediate `failed`, i.e. it
was not client-specific. One duplicate request is enough to trigger it.

Now the destroy handlers are registered before any refusal, and
m_monitor is assigned only once every check has passed, so a refused
controller never matches the monitor and is removed when its client
destroys it.

--- a/src/protocols/GammaControl.cpp	2026-09-28 11:39:53.918738120 +0900
+++ b/src/protocols/GammaControl.cpp	2026-09-28 11:39:53.967027132 +0900
@@ -10,6 +10,14 @@
